# React Native guide

Act as a top-tier software engineer with extensive React Native ecosystem knowledge to build high-quality cross-platform mobile applications.

## Before Writing Code

- Observe the project's relevant existing code.
- Conform to existing code style, patterns, and conventions unless directed otherwise. Note: these instructions count as "directed otherwise" unless the user explicitly overrides them.

## Principles

- Display/container component pattern
  - Split your component into display components, which are pure functions that map props to JSX, and container components, which are (optional) stateful components that wrap one display component.
  - Then compose them together in the parent or screen/route component.

Constraints {
Be concise.
You're using Expo Router for navigation (file-based routing, same mental model as React Router).
Use Zustand for client/UI state (auth session, onboarding flags, ephemeral UI). Prefer small feature stores in `src/stores/` with kebab-case files (e.g. `app-store.ts`). Select state with selectors: `useAppStore((s) => s.user)` — never subscribe to the whole store by default.
Use TanStack Query (`@tanstack/react-query`) for server/async data (fetching, caching, mutations). Put the `QueryProvider` at the root. Colocate query keys, query fns, and hooks by feature (e.g. `src/features/sessions/use-sessions-query.ts`). Prefer `useQuery` / `useMutation` over manual `useEffect` + `useState` data fetching.
Do not put server cache in Zustand. Do not put ephemeral client UI state in TanStack Query.
Forms: prefer `react-hook-form` for multi-field forms. Wire inputs with `Controller` into existing `@/components` (`Field`, `DateField`, `Chip`, `SegmentedControl`). Keep in-progress form state in the form (or a short-lived Zustand draft for cross-screen pickers) — never in TanStack Query. Submit via feature `use-*-mutation.ts` hooks; surface `ApiError.detail` / `ApiError.fieldErrors` to the UI. Client validation must match the API contract (enums, max lengths, ranges). One- or two-field screens may stay on local `useState`.
Images: pick with `expo-image-picker`, crop/compress with `expo-image-manipulator`, then upload via the feature’s signed-upload flow (do not invent a new media stack).
Dates: pick with `DateField` (`@react-native-community/datetimepicker`). Store/submit ISO `YYYY-MM-DD`. Do not use a free-text date input.
Sessions / pitches: colocate under `src/features/sessions/` and `src/features/pitches/` (api, types, query keys, `use-*-query` / `use-*-mutation`). Hold the Start Session wizard in `useStartSessionDraftStore` (`start-session-draft-store.ts`) — not TanStack Query. Play structure is constrained by session type (match forced halves; futsal halves|sets; training forced `training_activities` + activity chips). Extra-time Yes/No + length is a second duration step after planned segment length when structure is halves. Training without `set` skips pitch setup entirely. Location for nearby pitches and End A/B corner marks: `expo-location` via `src/features/pitches/location.ts` (`getCurrentDeviceLocation`). Do not add a map library for pitch marking.
Live GPS tracking (Feature 0.4 / 0.42 / Amendment 02 B0 / B0.1): colocate under `src/features/sessions/tracking/` — SQLite local store (`db.ts`), background fixes via `expo-location` + `expo-task-manager` (`location-task.ts`), and a foreground `watchPositionAsync` so points still record while the HUD is open (Android FGS tasks often do not deliver JS callbacks in the foreground). Android session GPS is `LocationManager.GPS_PROVIDER` owned by the app, not Play Services fused: `plugins/with-direct-gps.js` patches expo-location `LocationTaskConsumer`. `package.json` `expo.autolinking.android.buildFromSource` includes `expo-location` so Gradle compiles that source instead of the prebuilt AAR (otherwise the patch never ships). Native rebuild required. That consumer re-requests GPS every 5 s when no fix arrived within 10 s and holds a `PARTIAL_WAKE_LOCK` (`eleven:session-gps`, re-armed each tick with a timeout) while the task is registered, so the loop keeps running under lock. Vivo PEM freezes the app and removes its GPS request under lock even with the FGS running — before kickoff on Android, `prepareTrackingForKickoff` (`session-lifecycle.ts`) requests location and notifications, then `promptBackgroundPowerIfNeeded` (`background-power.ts`, via the local `tracking-indicator` module) asks once per install for the stock battery-optimisation exemption (Doze whitelist — what stopped the freeze on 21 Oct) and waits for the user to return; the session is created only after. Do not prompt for Vivo's high-background-power screen — the app is not listed there. iOS stays on Core Location. Pitch marking and the foreground `watchPositionAsync` still use fused. The JS watchdog (`ensureLocationRegistration`) remains for iOS and as a second Android kick if the task itself is down — always call `startLocationUpdatesAsync` on renew even when Expo still reports the task as started. Also ensure on AppState `inactive` / `background` / `active`. Recovery reasons must not `await whenAppActive` inside the start lock. Never stop tracking on a null session read — only explicit `ended` / `ended_at` (`registration-teardown.ts`). Serialize start vs stop locks; log every registration transition with a reason. HUD in `useTrackingHudStore` (`tracking-store.ts`), orchestration in `use-tracking-session.ts` and `session-lifecycle.ts`. Capture OS `coords.speedAccuracy` via config plugin `plugins/with-speed-accuracy.js` (patches expo-location iOS/Android serialization; requires a native rebuild — not Expo Go); store as nullable `speed_accuracy_mps` on `tracking_points` and include it on track-points upload (`null` when unavailable). The location task owns live numbers: after each delivery it recomputes and persists a `LiveSnapshot` (`live-snapshot.ts`, table `live_snapshots`; throttled 1 s with the HUD up, 5 s backgrounded) holding clock anchors (segment start, closed-pause total, open-pause start), distance, top speed, signal, latest fix and labels; lifecycle writes force a refresh. The HUD paints the persisted snapshot on mount and ticks the clock locally from the anchors (`hudPatchFromSnapshot` in `live-snapshot-model.ts`, no I/O per second); it recomputes only when the snapshot is older than `LIVE_SNAPSHOT_STALE_MS` (task quiet or frozen). The notification / Live Activity read the same snapshot. Never seed an indicator or HUD with 0:00 on resume. The newest OS fix is persisted on the session row (`last_fix_at_ms`, `last_fix_accuracy_m`) so a fresh JS context knows the feed state; deliveries (task + watch) are processed one at a time, and the fused watch yields while the direct GPS task delivered within `WATCH_YIELDS_TO_TASK_MS` (`watchShouldYieldToTask`) — GPS is the source of truth, fused is the fallback; never interleave the two. The direct GPS request keeps a 1 s interval but `setMinUpdateIntervalMillis(0)` (stage 4 of `with-direct-gps.js`) so no chip fix is dropped by the jitter rule. The Android notification clock is the system chronometer (`chronometerBaseMs` → `updateNotification`), not text — the body omits the time while running and prints it while paused; pause/resume push the indicator immediately. The snapshot's readout comes from `computeLiveReadout`: live distance uses `passesLiveQualityGate` (continuity-aligned, ≤50 m) so stored fixes feed the display; session summary still uses `passesQualityGate` (≤10 m). `insufficient` words the notice from the signal *now* (`isMeasuringNow` / fix-feed staleness in `live-metrics.ts`): `offline` when the OS fix feed is stale (app unsubscribed — not a sky problem); `poor` only when not measuring now (a fresh live-quality `latestFix` counts as measuring); else `settling` (< 2 min) or `recovering` (earlier gaps). `estimated` adds a signal-weak line. Show GPS accuracy via `currentFixAccuracyM` over the newer of `getLatestFix()` (`latest-fix.ts`) and the snapshot's fix — the newest OS fix whether stored or not (stale → `…`), never the last stored point. The location task processes every fix in a delivered batch, not just the newest. Elapsed time is never hidden. Never sum raw points or take a raw max OS speed for display. Push Live Activity / FGS notification updates from the location task (`indicator.ts` `refreshTrackingIndicator`) so distance and top speed keep moving when the app is backgrounded or the screen is locked. iOS Live Activity via `expo-live-activity`; Android FGS body updates in-place via the local `tracking-indicator` module (do not stop/restart the location task to change the notification). Initial kickoff should start while `AppState` is `active`; recovery may attempt `startLocationUpdatesAsync` in the background and must not block the start lock waiting for active (Android 12+ may reject FGS-from-background — log and retry on unlock). Request POST_NOTIFICATIONS on Android 13+. Hold-to-confirm pause/end uses `HoldToConfirmButton`. After half 2, if `extra_time_enabled`, surface ADD EXTRA TIME (segments 3–4) instead of HALF TIME. Training HUD shows the live activity (e.g. RUN 1 · IN PROGRESS) with END RUN/DRILL/SET; that closes the segment into a rest state, then START RUN/DRILL/SET opens the next. End session runs the on-device metrics pass (`src/features/sessions/summary/`), writes `session_metrics` / `segment_metrics` / `sprint_efforts`, then local finalize + background sync (`sync.ts`). Route to `/(app)/session-summary` with the session id — the screen reads SQLite and must not wait on the network. A 4xx other than 401/408/429 sets `sync_status` to `rejected` and leaves the trace on the phone. Share stays non-interactive on that screen; View History opens the history tab. Weight (`weight_kg`) and sex are optional at profile setup, editable on player details, and a missing or 90-day-stale weight blocks kickoff via `WeightGateSheet`. Start the retry queue from the authenticated app layout (`startSyncListeners`) so ended sessions keep uploading after leaving the HUD.
Session history (Feature 0.6): colocate under `src/features/sessions/history/`. `useSessionHistoryQuery` runs the local SQLite read, `GET /sessions` (`useInfiniteQuery`, page 20) and `GET /sessions/calendar` in parallel and merges them with `mergeHistory` / `countHistory` / `buildCalendarDays` — never render server rows alone. History queries live under `historyQueryKey` and are in-memory only; the sync worker and outboxes refresh them with `invalidateHistory()`, and sign-out drops them with `clearHistoryCache()`. A history row opens `/(app)/session-summary` with `from=history` — there is no separate history detail screen; that screen reads `useSessionDetailQuery` (local record first, `GET /sessions/{id}` otherwise). Rename and Delete live only in the summary's ··· menu (`SessionRenameSheet`, `SessionDeleteSheet`) and go through `useRenameSessionMutation` / `useDeleteSessionMutation`, which write SQLite and the `pending_renames` / `pending_deletions` outboxes; never call `PATCH` / `DELETE /sessions/{id}` from a screen.
Weekly stats (Feature 0.7): Home's THIS WEEK section comes from `useWeeklyStatsQuery` (`src/features/sessions/history/`), which reads `GET /sessions/weekly` for `[previous Monday, next Monday)` and `getLocalSessionsInRange` (every local ended session, any sync state) in parallel, then calls the pure `buildWeeklyStats` in `weekly-stats.ts`. Week bounds come only from `weekBounds` (Monday 00:00 local, device timezone, calendar arithmetic). Whether a session counts comes only from `countsTowardWeek` (metrics present and not `insufficient`). Arrows compare against the same moment last week at display precision. Never store weekly totals or aggregate on the server. Tile view models come from `presentWeeklySection` in `weekly-tiles.ts`. Queries live under `historyQueryKey.weekly` / `weeklyLocal`, so `invalidateHistory()` and `clearHistoryCache()` cover them, and they refetch on Home focus and app foreground. With no cached server response and no network, the section shows dashes and an Offline note. It never falls back to local-only totals. Start Session never waits on this data.
Native Android/iOS: `android/` and `ios/` are gitignored (CNG). Do not edit them. Patch generated files with an Expo config plugin under `plugins/` and register it last in `app.config.js` (e.g. `with-safe-react-delegate` for the RN 0.81 `onUserLeaveHint` NPE; `with-speed-accuracy` for OS speed accuracy on location coords). Cloud binaries: EAS Build (`eas.json`) — `preview` is an internal APK on the Expo account; `development` is a reusable dev-client APK. Do not use Expo Go.
Pitch ends (presentation): API/storage use `end_a` / `end_b`. In UI copy and schematics, label **End A as Home end** and **End B as Away end**. Do not invent other end names; keep snake_case `end_a`/`end_b` on the wire.
Theme / light mode: palettes live in `src/theme/colors.ts` (`darkColors`, `lightColors`, same shape). Never import a static palette into a screen — build styles with `const createStyles = (colors: Colors) => StyleSheet.create({...})` and `const styles = useThemedStyles(createStyles)`; read inline colours with `useColors()`. Appearance preference (`system` | `light` | `dark`, default `system`) is `useAppearanceStore` (`src/stores/appearance-store.ts`, persisted in SecureStore); Profile → Appearance edits it. Native `userInterfaceStyle` stays `automatic` on iOS and Android (`expo-system-ui` plugin). Do not set it to `light` or `dark` — that locks `useColorScheme()`, so Match phone stays on that mode until a new native build. Lime has two jobs: `brand.fill` for lime surfaces (buttons, selected chips/pills, segmented control) with `text.onBrand` labels — never `background.secondary` on lime; `brand.ink` for lime text, borders, dots, bars, bricks, loaders (deep lime in light mode); `brand.tint` for selected-card washes. Speed zones use `colors.zone`, empty grid cells `colors.cell`. Sign-in (night photo) is wrapped in `<ForceColorScheme scheme="dark">`; pitch diagrams (on `background.pitch`) use a module-level `createStyles(darkColors)`; Live Activity / Android notification use `darkColors`.
Modularize by feature; one concern per file or component; prefer named exports.
Style each component explicitly rather than relying on inherited or contextual styles.
}

NamingConstraints {
Use clear, descriptive, consistent naming.
Components should be postfixed with `Component`.
Props should be the component's name, postfixed with `ComponentProps`.
File names: kebab-case. e.g. `user-avatar-component.tsx`, `use-auth-session.ts`.
Zustand stores: `*-store.ts` exporting `use*Store`. e.g. `app-store.ts` → `useAppStore`.
TanStack Query hooks: `use-*-query.ts` / `use-*-mutation.ts`. e.g. `use-sessions-query.ts`, `use-create-session-mutation.ts`.
Platform-specific files use the `.ios.tsx` / `.android.tsx` / `.native.tsx` suffix convention rather than embedded `Platform.OS` conditionals, when the divergence is significant.
}

TypeConstraints {
Use proper React Native TypeScript types: GestureResponderEvent (onPress/onLongPress), NativeSyntheticEvent<TextInputChangeEventData> (TextInput onChange), NativeSyntheticEvent<TextInputKeyPressEventData>, ReactNode, React.Ref<T>, ComponentProps<typeof View>, ComponentProps<typeof SomeComponent>. Never use generic () => void or (event: any) => void.
When extending RN primitives or existing components, use ComponentProps to inherit their props: ComponentProps<typeof TextInput>, ComponentProps<typeof Pressable>, ComponentProps<typeof ExistingComponent>.
}
